meta {
  name: Log Campaign Mailer Lead
  type: http
  seq: 16
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/mailers/log-lead
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "controlNumber": "9c41b2d7ee01"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setVar("campaignMailerLeadId", res.getBody().leadId);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    // 200, not 201: resolve-or-create. On a re-run of the collection the lead
    // from the previous run is still linked to this mailer, and that is the
    // correct answer rather than a second lead.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("one agency now owns this mailer", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.leadId).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.alreadyExisted).to.be.a("boolean");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /mailers/log-lead — the campaign's own mailer, from the demo agency

  The same endpoint the **Mailers** folder covers, aimed at a mailer this
  folder's campaign just imported rather than at the demo seed's. It exists to
  set up the two requests after it: **one lead per mailer is platform-wide**
  (PAC-71 open item 7), so proving that needs a mailer *two* agencies can see,
  and only a campaign committed with `assignment.mode: "all"` produces one.

  - **Module:** `mailers` **and** `leads`
  - **Permission:** `mailers:read` + `leads:write`
  - **Data scope:** the lead is created in the **caller's** agency — the mailer
    is shared, the lead never is.

  Sent as the demo producer (`{{accessToken}}`, from **Auth › Login**), because
  the platform operator this folder otherwise uses holds no module permissions
  at all and would 403 here.

  `9c41b2d7ee01` is the short printed form of
  `#7c1e4a90-1111-4b8a-9f10-9c41b2d7ee01`, the fixture row **List Campaign
  Records** searches for. Either form normalizes to the same
  `controlNumberKeys` entry, so which one is sent is a display detail.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  { "leadId": "…", "alreadyExisted": false }
  ```

  `alreadyExisted` is deliberately not asserted to a fixed value: it is `false`
  on a database seeded fresh and `true` on every re-run, and both are correct.
  What matters for the next two requests is only that *some* lead in the demo
  agency now owns this mailer.
}
